 on the  allegation that this was i think the  words are being used our dog whistle  uh to muslim voters that um  actually the tories are in some way  anti-muslim  and that they identify with um what is  said to be an anti-muslim government  led by mr modi there's none of that  going on here  i don't um accept that um that  that that insinuation um trevor and what  we saw  in batley and spain was underhand  tactics  by galloway and his campaign bringing  people from outside to whip up that  hatred and  division with actual violence and  aggression towards the labour candidate  harassing intimidating chasing her down  the street and also to labour  activists that is the true nature of  what was happening  in batley and spain but the people of  that community rose above that  and returned kim as their mp last week  that is a great result  for labour it's also a great result for  batley and sten  all right let's talk about what you want  to talk about today which is the pro  your promise that the labor government  would make buy and sell  more in britain and that you'd bring  more jobs home  is this some an update to gordon brown's  pledge  of british jobs for british workers  it's very different from that this is  about a post-pandemic recovery plan  and how as chancellor i would ensure  that we support  high street small businesses and jobs  and industry  across the united kingdom as we address  some of the big challenges that we  face as a country and i put together a  three-point plan  to buy make and sell more in britain  and that includes using all the tools at  government's disposal  to do so so for example with procurement  to use stretching targets in procurement  to  award more contracts to companies  creating jobs and skills and  apprenticeships here in britain  to help businesses who want  post-pandemic and also post-brexit  to reassure jobs to make their supply  chains  more secure and resilient to help them  to do that  and also to sort out the big gaping  holes  in the brexit deal that the government  signed last year  to make it easier for british industries  and workers to export goods and services  across the european union  especially in our creative industries  professional services  and in farming and fisheries as well and  with that three-point plan  we can ensure that we're buying making  and selling more in this country  ensuring that the uk economy is firing  on  all cylinders and that we are more  secure and more resilient  in face of the sorts of shocks that any  economy  and especially one that's been through  everything we've been through  in the last year and a half to ensure  that we are more secure and resilient  in the face of those shocks i i think  this is what um distinguished economies  economists like yourself call a  non-tariff barrier so let's take an  example  and see how this would work in reality  biggest construction project in the  country pretty much uh hs2 high-speed  line um six months ago the bosses of hs2  picked a small group of construction  companies uh  to compete for contracts worth two and a  half  billion pounds for new rail lines now  just  one of the companies they picked is  british  would your proposals mean that hs2  when they make their decision would be  more likely  to pick the british company  i think that's a really good example  trevor because whether or not you think  that hs2  should go ahead and i know there are big  differences of opinion on that  i think we can all agree that that  investment should benefit  jobs skills apprenticeships and  businesses here  in britain because it is in our national  interest to create those high-skill  well-paid jobs and what my proposals  that i'm  i'm setting out today of how i would  approach this as chancellor of the  exchequer  would say that when you look at the  different bids that come in for any  project whether it be hs2 or offshore  wind or whatever it  is you can look at the plans of the  businesses bidding for that contract  to see what impact it would have on the  wider uk economy what strategic  value a bid has and if it is creating  jobs  in this country high skilled well-paid  jobs and apprenticeships  and that means that we're building up  that skill set so we can export around  the world  those bids should get more weight than  bids that are creating no jobs no skills  and no investment  in this country so it's not saying that  we won't give  um contracts to to foreign companies  absolutely not but it is saying  like in france and like in the united  states we would take into account  the social impact of any public  procurement  other countries do that because they  know it is strategically important  and yet in the uk we don't use those  powers that we have at our disposal and  that is a mistake and i would do that  differently  so you you say this is a good example um  so as chancellor what you would do is  create an environment in which  the in that case the british company  balfour beatty would  be favored so you would be leaning on  hs2 bosses  toward the contract to a company  implicated in the 2005 hatfield rail  crash  a company whose management has been  black was blacklisted  because uh it uh because it blacklisted  trade union  activists and still faces legal actions  from some unions  and the company which in 2019 was  suspended  for from bidding for government work  because it consistently made payments to  small and medium-sized  businesses late is that sort of company  that labor wants to see getting the work  because it's got a union jack on its  website  trevor it wouldn't be me as chancellor  leaning on  public bodies to award contracts what it  is about is  changing the procurement framework so  you can take into account  labor standards job creation in this  country and  environmental standards as well so there  is a level playing field  for businesses doing business in this  country and we take into account the  whole value  of the bids including the impact it  would have  on the environment on labour standards  and on jobs in this country  as i say under w2lto rules  under the trade agreement that we did  with our european partners last year  these things are possible  other countries do it because i know it  is strategically important  we should as well and that would ensure  that we create  more good jobs for small businesses and  large businesses  here in britain and that is the right  thing to do  it's not what is happening at the moment  in a whole range of government contracts  whether it is  offshore wind or hs2 or trident  submarines  we need to do things differently because  we have to grow the industries of the  future and ensure that there are more  good quality high-pay jobs in every  corner  of our country and as chancellor of the  exchequer  i would use every tool at my disposal to  ensure that the uk economy is firing on  all cylinders  and creating more good quality jobs that  you can raise the family on  just yeah so one very quick last  question this is your first  big announcement says shadow chancellor  your predecessor  uh john mcdonnell said that you can't  understand capitalism  without reading karl marx's dash capital  have you had that privilege  i haven't trevor had that privilege of  um reading  uh it but i studied economics for my  degree and for my master's degree i've  worked for many years  at the bank of england at the british  embassy in washington as an economist  and for halifax bank of scotland so  i hope my credentials speak for  themselves and as chancellor  i would do things differently but always  ensuring  that our policies are costed that they  add up and that they are in the best  interests  of the british people okay i'm sure mr  mcdonald's got a spare copy to lend you  if you  want it rachel reeves thank you very  much

